WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.334
Detections are not one-time creations.

00:00:02.334 --> 00:00:06.357
Systems change, technologies evolve, threats adapt, and organizations mature.

00:00:06.357 --> 00:00:11.272
A detection that was useful last year may become noisy, stale,

00:00:11.272 --> 00:00:13.507
or incomplete as conditions change.

00:00:13.507 --> 00:00:17.529
The detection lifecycle includes design, implementation, testing, deployment, monitoring,

00:00:17.529 --> 00:00:19.317
tuning, documentation, and retirement.

00:00:19.317 --> 00:00:26.467
Each stage should preserve context so future maintainers understand the purpose and assumptions behind the detection.

00:00:26.467 --> 00:00:30.042
Maintenance should be based on feedback and evidence.

00:00:30.042 --> 00:00:33.617
Alert outcomes, analyst comments, incident lessons, telemetry changes,

00:00:33.617 --> 00:00:39.427
and business context can all show whether a detection needs tuning, enrichment, suppression,

00:00:39.427 --> 00:00:40.321
or replacement.

00:00:40.321 --> 00:00:43.002
A sustainable program makes review routine.

00:00:43.002 --> 00:00:47.471
Detections should have owners, review dates, clear logic, expected outputs,

00:00:47.471 --> 00:00:51.940
and criteria for when they should be improved or retired.
